essarily activists they  were just uh university of havana  students that like young professionals  and i asked them you know what do you  what do you think because by way of  context most people in cuba recognize  uh the failings of the regime they they  recognize and they'll speak  quietly and in hush tones uh about  um you know the need for change but they  don't really want to speak out and i  asked these young cubans  what do you hope for and almost to a  person they said we hope to leave  we have no future here and that's very  difficult and what's interesting now  is suddenly is that sense of desperation  that lack of of hope  may suddenly be being challenged to the  street rather than an effort to flee  and that's positive and i think once you  build that once you build that sense of  of hope then you get what what your  article says as people start to speak  about  the lack of fear they're not as afraid  uh and i think that's very very  important in terms of  pockets of support for the regime you  know it's very difficult to tell of  course there are people in the  government  who very much support support the regime  but you never know  i mean as i when i teach at columbia  university  i would tell my students you know try  and describe what it's like in cuba and  the conversations you have privately  with people  you know imagine working in an office  where the same boss has been in power  for 60 years  how frustrating would that be you know  that person just  personally controls everything that  happens in the office and it depends on  on his whim uh would not be a work  environment that you'd want to work in  so we don't even know within the  government how much they support this  and lastly is one other element to this  which is very different  which is there's not a caster on power  right now last year raul castro stepped  down for the presidency of the  communist party before that he stepped  down from the presidency of the country  and this is going