myself if i missed this  my dad went to the  1966 world cup final at wembley so i  feel like i ought to be at this one  and so on and so forth but it did mean  that when i went to the game my  conscience was  gnawing away at me throughout so did  your  son accept your argument and your point  of view that it just runs in the family  that  fathers go to uh finals alone  um i i'm not sure whether he's accepted  it yet  um he says that he doesn't mind and  doesn't blame me and didn't want me to  turn it down but i think only time will  tell whether he really has forgiven me  yeah toby it's the kind of thing you'll  find out on his wedding day he's still  holding on to when his wedding speech  only centers on that one topic but as  you say in your piece perhaps it was  good that your son wasn't there talk to  us about what it was like at wembley  well it was all pretty hairy  particularly getting in  so as me and my two companions arrived  hundreds of fans broke through the metal  barrier which you had to kind of stand  outside in order to  show your covert pass as you then enter  the kind of perimeter of the stadium so  they broke through this barrier  and stormed through the handicap  entrance of  wembley and then stormed into the  stadium ticketless fans  and and then um other fans took  advantage of the chaos in the wake of  this  barrier break to swarm through the  barriers themselves and  around all the gated entrances and  people were kind of  queueing up they were shoved out of the  way in some cases people holding  phones because everyone had their  tickets downloaded on their phones  had their phones snatched out of their  hands so these ticketless fans could  then get past the turnstiles and  into the grounds and into seats it was  really chaotic and  even as we got through the turnstile  ourselves um  my my companion was knocked over in a  sort of malay as  there was a sort of mass brawl like a  scene in a kind of western bar  between these kind of marauding  hooligans and the wembley stewards the  police were nowhere in sight  and my friend got knocked over and he  lost his phone in the process and  someone immediately grabbed it and  started running away  at which point another fan rugby tackled  the thief and then he was able to get  his phone  back but it was like if my 13 year old  son had been there i'm not sure  um it would have been ideal you know i  would have been very concerned not just  about protecting my phone but about  protecting my son as well i mean it was  it wasn't really a suitable occasion for  families to be honest  no it doesn't sound like a family  occasion at all and the more reports  that are coming out it seems like  security  uh was just not prepared for what they  were actually going to be  facing i mean did you feel safe when you  were there did you feel like the  security was lacking  it felt as though the security was set  up so if you  had a covered pass and you had a ticket  it was incredibly difficult to get into  the stadium  but if you hadn't bothered to get  vaccinated or taken a lateral flow test  and you didn't have a ticket  there was nothing to prevent you kind of  rushing into the stadium it was you know  for law-abiding citizens it was  unbelievably torturous  and lengthy the process to get in but if  you were a hooligan  you know you just waltzed on it it was  it was it was really poorly  uh uh uh um uh arranged managed and  there were some police there but um they  were completely overwhelmed and  outnumbered i don't think they were  expecting  so many fans to converge on wembley  stadium i mean  maybe it was a silly idea to hold it at  eight o'clock in the evening given that  then  a lot of fans would have been drinking  all day and a lot of the people kind of  running amok  in the stadium without tickets trying to  steal people's phones were clearly drunk  yeah oh gosh i mean to be honest um well  i desperately wish i had been there it  sounds like maybe maybe i'm glad i  wasn't  um the game started well enough anyway  was all your anxiety swept away within  two minutes when luke shaw put away that  goal  yeah no i wouldn't say it was i mean i i  um you know as soon as  we put the kind of um mayhem behind us  and settled into our seats we then kind  of became  really anxious about what was in front  of us which was you know  um the first international tournament  final  england's been in for 55 years and you  know football's  nerve-wracking enough at the best of  times when the stakes are that  high it becomes almost unbearably tense  um and um i think if england had got a  goal you know  in the second half or even on you know  uh just before half time  that would have done something to  relieve the tension but getting a goal  so quickly and then  sitting back in our own half and sort of  inviting the italians to attack our goal  and doing our best to defend it that  actually just ratcheted up the anxiety  and i became  absolutely convinced that the italians  were going to equalise and then as soon  as they did equalize i thought well  that's it  it's going to penalties it was always  going to go to penalties  and we're going to lose the penalty  shootout as we always do it seemed like  a kind of  the tragedy of english football playing  itself out in front of us and there was  just  nothing you could do about it i mean the  fans were willing the team to succeed  roaring them on applauding the slightest  exhibition of talent uh but kind of  knowing in their heart of hearts all  sixty 000 of us that there was really  nothing we could do  to escape the kind of ghost of england  football past and you know if it came to  a penalty shootout we'd just be bound to  lose because we always do  there was this kind of you know naive  belief that because it was a young  team and they seemed so optimistic and  well managed  by gareth southgate that they would be  able to kind of cast off  these demons you know they wouldn't be  haunted by the ghost of summer's past  you know would they be able to bring  football home uh but actually  turned out when it came down to it uh in  the penalty shootout  suddenly the ghosts were there on the  pitch kind of on the player's shoulders  whispering in their ears  and and of course they they fluffed it  well our viewers are painfully aware of  how that story  ended this is my last question to you do  you think the disappointment of losing  and the messiness at wembley was worth  the joys of beating germany  winning against denmark in extra time  and making it to the first final in over  50 years  yeah it was um it was a fantastic  tournament and  um you know a huge um uh tonic  after 16 months of kind of misery  and i enjoyed i enjoyed it i mean i  couldn't i didn't quite enter into  i wasn't as enthusiastic about it as i  might have been and i wrote about that  in the spectator a few weeks ago  i had various reservations about the  kind of  progressive strain running through the  england setup  and the kind of various gestures they  were making including taking the knee  which i wasn't particularly  enthusiastic about i wanted them to to  come together with another less  divisive um way of signaling their  support for the cause of anti-racism  other than taking the knee which is so  indelibly wrapped up with black lives  matter but you know  i got i got you know i i i got beyond  that and when we scored  you know when kane scored the winning  goal against denmark suddenly i was all  in and just  hoping against hope that we'd win  um but you know it's the hope that kills  you toby  thanks for joining us and that's all for  this week  if you enjoyed this episode why not  subscribe to the spectators youtube  channel so you never miss out  just click the subscribe button at the  bottom of this video and the bell icon  to make sure you never  miss an episode thanks again for  watching the show  and do join us again next week on  spectator tv  [Music]  you
 

